FWIW, the reason i said that i don't know how it works
is that, even assuming twos complement, ~0 may very well
be -1; which is what Python (where integers can grow as
big as your memory can handle) does:

>>> ~0
-1

They're equivalent at a bitwise level (again, assuming
twos complement, -1 might as well be equivalent to the
maximum representable unsigned integer modulo 2^{size}),
so there is technically no reason to prefer one over the
other.  Python only has one choice to use -1 since there
is no "largest integer"; what i am less confident about
though is perl, since (2^{size}-1) (UV) and -1 both seem
to be viable candidates for the all-1 mask:

$ perl -lwe 'print ~0 & 777'
777
$ perl -lwe 'print -1 & 777'
777

The only thing stopping ~0 from being -1 is the interpre-
tation of the most significant bit as signed/unsigned...
